{
  "result": "There was a time when I thought interesting people were the ones who had seen the most extraordinary things. Now I think the interesting ones are those who find extraordinary things in whatever they've seen.\n\nI learned this from an old repairman who came to fix my refrigerator. He spent an hour in my kitchen, and while he worked he narrated the secret life of dust, how it collects behind appliances like snowdrifts, how every house has its own weather system. He held up a rusted coil and described it like an archaeologist holding a bone.\n\nSince then I've been trying to practice his kind of attention. To notice not just the big events, but the textures in between.\n\nThe way morning light hits the steam from a mug and makes it briefly visible, then invisible again. The particular squeak of my favorite chair that only happens when I lean left. The way strangers on the bus hold their phones - some like delicate birds, some like hammers.\n\nThis isn't nostalgia or mindfulness as performance. It's closer to gratitude without needing to name it. It's understanding that the world is constantly offering you small, free exhibits, and most of us walk past with our heads down, scrolling.\n\nNothing has changed about my life since I started noticing. Everything has changed.\n\nIf you're looking for a place to start, start with light. Follow it for a day. Watch where it lands. You'll be amazed what has been waiting to be seen.",
